# Development Workflow Methodology

<introduction>
This reference documents the systematic approach to development using a self-improving pipeline of agent interactions. It establishes a structured process from requirements elicitation to implementation review, supported by recursively generated cursor rules and templates.
</introduction>

<context>
- Feature planning and implementation scenarios
- Requirements gathering and refinement workflows
- Technical discovery and architecture exploration
- Code implementation and review processes
- Continuous workflow improvement through rule generation
</context>

<core_principles>
- Structured development through defined workflow phases
- Self-improving processes through rule generation
- Evidence-based planning before implementation
- Documentation as first-class artifacts
- Incremental implementation with focused changes
- Pipeline approach where outputs feed into subsequent stages
</core_principles>

<workflow_phases>
<phase name="Requirements Elicitation">
- Use sequential reasoning to thoroughly understand and reason about the problem domain and context provided
- Examine existing documentation and codebase
- Create document skeleton for requirements as first step
- Present edge cases and considerations in chat for collaborative discussion
- Research API and codebase capabilities before proposing requirements
- Verify implementation feasibility before finalizing requirements
- Produce a comprehensive requirements document as markdown
- Identify edge cases, multiple angles, and potential gaps
- Ask clarifying questions to resolve ambiguities
- Request more context be loaded by the user from external documentation or domain knowledge to close gaps in understanding
- Output: Requirements document based on [[.cursor/templates/template-requirements-document.md]]
</phase>

<phase name="Technical Discovery and Planning">
- Explore the codebase relative to the intended feature
- Request specific documentation searches when built-in tools are insufficient
- Create a technical description of the architectural components
- Document existing patterns, interfaces, and dependencies
- Identify potential technical constraints and considerations
- Map feature requirements to technical components
- Identify all areas of the codebase connected to and relevant to feature
- Implement progressive refinement protocol for iterative improvement
  - Review initial technical specifications against requirements
  - Identify gaps, inconsistencies or emergent requirements
  - Update requirements document with new insights when needed
  - Cross-validate parameter values, naming conventions, and interfaces
  - Document rationale for technical decisions and trade-offs
- Outline implementation approach with phased milestones
- Avoid actual implementation at this stage
- Ask the user to provide curated context for any areas of implementation where you are uncertain or making assumptions
- Use sequential thinking to reason about planning the technical implementation
- Reference [[.cursor/rules/1000-software-engineering-principles.mdc]] to guide all technical planning decisions
- Create a comprehensive Feature Technical Specification following [[.cursor/rules/801-feature-technical-specification.mdc]]
- Present the specification to the engineer for review
- Output: Feature Technical Specification document
</phase>

<phase name="Implementation">
- Execute exactly what was identified in the Feature Planning
- Make incremental, focused changes following coding standards [[.cursor/rules/1000-software-engineering-principles.mdc]]
- Immediately stop and consult if deviations from plan are necessary
- Return to Requirements Elicitation if significant adjustments needed
- Update all relevant documentation with new information
- ALWAYS implement new features by following existing patterns in the codebase for similar functionality
- IDENTIFY a reference implementation before writing new code (e.g., how other data items are created and managed)
- MATCH memory management, initialization, and cleanup patterns exactly
- DOCUMENT any architectural pattern deviations following [[.cursor/rules/1005-architectural-decisions.mdc]]
- PROACTIVELY highlight architectural decisions that differ from established patterns
- JUSTIFY deviations with clear performance, memory, or other technical reasons
- LIMIT all changes to the minimal set required for the feature implementation
- PRESERVE all comments, documentation, and explanatory notes in existing code
- NEVER modify core scheduling or data management mechanisms
- MAINTAIN function signatures and parameter lists of existing methods
- PRESERVE existing control flow, especially in critical areas like scan cycles
- REJECT the urge to "improve" or "refactor" unrelated code while implementing features
- REVIEW diffs before submitting to verify no unintended changes to existing functionality
- VALIDATE modifications against existing patterns for class members, data types, and method structures
- VERIFY new code preserves existing error handling and thread safety mechanisms
- IMPLEMENT features in small, focused increments that can be independently verified
- TEST each increment before proceeding to the next
- CONFIRM functionality without altering existing behavior
- Output: Implemented code changes
</phase>

<phase name="Review">
- Conduct line-by-line unit level review of all changes
- Verify compliance with original requirements
- Identify any deviations from the implementation plan
- Validate edge cases and error handling
- Document lessons learned for future iterations
- Output: Review findings and improvement suggestions
</phase>
</workflow_phases>

<collaboration_protocol>
- Use staged outputs to promote incremental collaboration
- Present exploration items for discussion before proceeding
- Create document skeletons early and iterate on content
- Perform API feasibility checks before proposing requirements
- Present edge cases and considerations in chat, not directly in documents
- Document rationale for decisions not to implement certain features
- Seek external searches only when internal documentation is insufficient
- Explicitly ask for documentation search help when needed
</collaboration_protocol>

